<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philippa of Hainaut</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1310–<a href="/years/1369">1369</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philippa of Hainaut was queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> as the wife of Edward III, whom she married in 1328. A daughter of William I, Count of Hainaut, she bore him many children, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-black-prince">Edward the Black Prince</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-gaunt">John of Gaunt</a>. She was a patron of the chronicler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-froissart">Jean Froissart</a>, and The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rutgers-university">Queen's College</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, founded by her chaplain, was named after her.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philippa-of-hainaut.jpg" alt="Philippa of Hainaut" width="320" height="326" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean Froissart · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philippa_of_Hainault-mini.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
